  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "$$\n\n$$\n\nConsider the vector $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].$ By evaluating the following quadratic forms at $x$ **only,** determine which of them **cannot** be positive-definite.\n\n- $R(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+5{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$\n- $S(x)={x}^{T}\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}]x$\n- $T(x)={x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{1}{x}_{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "A quadratic form $Q(x)$ is positive-definite if $Q(x)>0$ for all $x\\in {R}^{n}\u2216{0}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the quadratic forms by substituting $x=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]:$\n\n- Quadratic form I *cannot* be positive-definite since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+5\\cdot 1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-10 \\\\  & =-5<0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form II *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 9\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =45>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Quadratic form III *might* be positive-definite. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T(x) & ={1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =1+4-2 \\\\  & =3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
